# TrueCost Elite Mobile UI/UX Deep Research and Design Blueprint

## What world-class mobile design actually looks like now

There is no useful single “world #1 UI app” that TrueCost should copy. The better benchmark is the intersection of **award-winning interaction, award-winning visuals, Apple’s current interface guidance, Google’s current Material 3 Expressive direction, accessibility standards, and the strongest patterns used by category-leading consumer products**.

Apple’s 2026 Design Awards are especially relevant because Apple evaluates apps across Delight & Fun, Inclusivity, Innovation, Interaction, Social Impact, and Visuals & Graphics. The current Interaction winner, **Moonlitt**, is praised for simple elegance, easy onboarding, and excellent integration of the new glass design language. **Tide Guide**, the Visuals & Graphics winner and an Interaction finalist, combines rich full-screen data visualization, custom animation, adaptive color, and large amounts of information while keeping the result crisp and understandable. **Structured** is specifically praised for being visually sharp and easy to scan. citeturn13view0

The important lesson is that “wow” does **not** mean putting blur, gradients, particles, 3D objects, animated icons, and glass on everything. Apple’s 2026 examples range from the very minimal Primary and Structured to visually expressive Tide Guide and (Not Boring) Camera. Their common trait is that the visual language supports the product’s purpose rather than competing with it. citeturn13view0

The 2025 Apple Design Awards reinforce the same principle. Taobao won Interaction partly for highly polished interactions and its genuinely useful side-by-side product comparison. Vocabulary was praised for consistent illustrations, balanced typography and iconography, and tasteful haptic feedback. Speechify’s design was recognized for reducing cognitive load rather than simply looking decorative. citeturn12search0

Google is moving in a compatible direction. Material 3 Expressive now emphasizes **emotion-driven UX, intuitive motion, adaptive components, flexible typography, contrasting shapes, and a more expressive use of color** rather than flat uniform surfaces. Google also distinguishes an **expressive motion scheme for prominent/hero interactions** from a **standard scheme for recurring utilitarian interactions**, which is an excellent model for TrueCost. citeturn16search8turn13view3

Google’s newer Gemini interfaces provide another especially relevant lesson for TrueCost: AI output does not have to remain a wall of conversational text. Gemini is increasingly rendering interactive visualizations and generative UI, while Google describes its new visual system as animating with purpose so attention remains focused on the task. That is precisely how TrueCost should present AI-driven decisions: **structured, visual, interactive results rather than a chat transcript with cards attached underneath**. citeturn19search3turn19search6

For the Indian market, Google Play named District its Best App of 2025 in India, highlighting personalized, locally relevant discovery. That reinforces that TrueCost should feel locally intelligent rather than like a Western design template with rupee symbols added afterward. citeturn13view2

### The design principles I would extract for TrueCost

The research points toward six principles:

**Clarity before decoration.** One screen should have one obvious visual priority.

**Expressiveness at important moments.** Search activation, comparison completion, basket optimization, and recommendation reveal deserve distinctive motion. Scrolling through 30 offers does not.

**Visual storytelling where the category benefits from it.** Food, travel, shopping, and selected home surfaces should be image-rich. Pharmacy and finance should be substantially more restrained.

**Glass as a functional layer rather than a wallpaper.** Apple explicitly recommends using Liquid Glass primarily for navigation and controls floating above content, warns against putting it throughout the content layer, and recommends using it sparingly. citeturn11search0turn11search2

**Trust must be visible.** A recommendation needs enough information for the user to understand why it won, which providers were considered, what is unknown, and how fresh the underlying information is.

**Motion has to improve understanding.** Apple specifically warns about excessive fast/blinking motion and recommends reduced-motion alternatives; Google similarly distinguishes prominent expressive motion from routine standard motion. citeturn15search0turn13view3

Your repository already has a valuable advantage: TrueCost is not one marketplace. Its target architecture spans **eight categories and 32 provider integrations**, with scrapers/connectors across Grocery, Food, Pharmacy, Shopping, Mobility, Travel, Finance, and Productivity/SaaS. The implementation depth varies by category, so the UI should share one design language while allowing substantially different decision experiences for each domain. fileciteturn0file0

That means the correct goal is **not**:

> “Make eight marketplaces inside one app.”

It is:

> **“Create one beautiful decision operating system whose visual expression changes intelligently with what the user is deciding.”**

## The visual language TrueCost should own

My recommendation is to develop a distinct design language called **TrueCost Luminous Intelligence**.

It would be a white-first, extremely refined visual system with selective glass, depth, bold typography, real imagery, premium motion, provider branding, and a very strong recommendation hierarchy.

### The white theme should feel expensive, not empty

Do not use pure `#FFFFFF` everywhere.

Use a layered white system conceptually like:

```text
Canvas / Porcelain
#F8F9FC

Primary Surface
#FFFFFF

Raised Surface
#FFFFFF with extremely soft elevation

Secondary Surface
#F3F5F8

Primary Ink
#10131A

Secondary Ink
#5D6472

Muted Ink
#8D94A3
```

The exact values should be finalized after testing on the actual emulator and physical OLED/LCD devices.

The reason for slightly tonal whites is visual separation without requiring a border around every card.

TrueCost then needs **one recognizable signature chromatic identity**. I would use an iris/violet intelligence accent rather than generic fintech blue:

```text
TrueCost Iris
approximately #6657F5

Intelligence Lavender
approximately #A99EFF

Value / Savings Mint
approximately #18A978

Positive Sea Glass
approximately #DDF7EF

Attention Amber
approximately #E9A23B

Critical / Error
controlled red family
```

Provider brand colors should **not** become the application palette. Blinkit yellow, Zepto purple, Zomato red, Amazon orange, Uber black, etc. should appear only within provider identity components and selected offer accents. This keeps the overall interface calm while allowing authentic brands to pop against the neutral TrueCost canvas.

### Glassmorphism: yes, but much less than you think

I strongly recommend glass, but specifically **functional glass**.

Apple’s current guidance treats Liquid Glass as a distinct layer for controls/navigation above content and explicitly warns that using glass throughout the content layer produces unnecessary complexity and a confusing hierarchy. Apple also reserves the most transparent version for rich photo/video backgrounds where the underlying media deserves to remain visible. citeturn11search0

Therefore TrueCost should use glass for things like:

```text
Floating bottom navigation
Universal search lens
Floating filter controls
Comparison sheet header
Transient chips
Selected interactive controls
Floating route controls over maps
Flight controls over destination imagery
Image-overlay actions
```

But **not**:

```text
Every product card
Every price row
Every fee breakdown
Every settings panel
Every medicine card
Every financial product
```

The result would feel contemporary without turning into a “Dribbble glassmorphism demo.”

For React Native/Expo, this is technically viable. Expo SDK 55 documents stable Android BlurView support; on Android 12/API 31+ it can use the more efficient RenderNode path, while older versions can require a less efficient fallback. Expo also warns that BlurView placement relative to dynamic content such as FlatList matters. citeturn15search1

So the production implementation should have:

```text
Glass-capable modern device
→ blur + translucent material

older/low-performance device
→ translucent opaque fallback
```

A user should never receive dropped frames just so we can say “we use glass.”

### Typography should become part of the brand

Apple describes typography as simultaneously serving legibility, information hierarchy, important-content communication, and brand expression. citeturn19search9

TrueCost needs a disciplined scale such as:

```text
Display        36–42
Hero           30–34
Page Title     26–30
Section        20–22
Card Title     16–18
Body           15–17
Supporting     13–14
Caption        11–13
```

Those are proposed design tokens, not platform minimums.

Prices should get their own optical treatment:

```text
₹1,249
```

rather than:

```text
INR 1249.0
```

And the app should use **very few weights**:

```text
Regular
Medium
Semibold
Bold only for exceptional emphasis
```

Ultra-light text on white/glass backgrounds would undermine legibility.

### Make the recommendation visually dominant

The defining TrueCost screen should not resemble:

```text
Provider A  ₹550
Provider B  ₹524
Provider C  ₹601
Provider D  unavailable
```

Instead, the top of the result should communicate the decision immediately:

```text
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ✦ BEST OVERALL                       │
│                                       │
│  [Blinkit]                            │
│                                       │
│  ₹524                                 │
│  TRUECOST                             │
│                                       │
│  Save ₹77                             │
│  versus the next complete option      │
│                                       │
│  ✓ All 8 items available              │
│  ✓ Updated 4 min ago                  │
│                                       │
│  Why this wins                  ›      │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Then:

```text
Other options
↓
Detailed comparison
↓
Cost breakdown
↓
Evidence/freshness
```

The best screen is one where the user can understand the recommendation in **roughly one glance**, while power users can progressively open the supporting details.

### Use expressive geometry rather than dozens of decorations

Material 3 Expressive’s current vocabulary includes flexible typography, contrasting shapes and adaptive components. citeturn16search8

TrueCost can translate that into its own language:

- Hero recommendation cards: larger 28–32px radius.
- Standard cards: 20–24px.
- Chips: pill/capsule.
- Search: oversized luminous capsule.
- Provider identity: compact rounded “brand tile.”
- Bottom sheets: large upper corner radius.
- Winner badge: asymmetrical or signature TrueCost shape.
- Category illustrations: soft organically curved crop frames.

This creates distinction without clutter.

## How the eight TrueCost categories should look and behave

The app should have **one shared shell and eight art directions**.

The Home screen, navigation, search, provider identity, typography, spacing, glass materials, sheets, buttons and recommendation architecture remain consistent. The content presentation then adapts to what the user is deciding.

| Category | Visual character | Primary visual | Comparison emphasis | Signature interaction |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Grocery | Fresh, fast, bright | Real packshots | Basket completeness + total | Items assemble into best basket |
| Food | Warm, editorial, appetizing | Dish/restaurant imagery | Delivered total + ETA | Menu card → provider comparison |
| Pharmacy | Calm, clinical, trustworthy | Product pack | Strength + form + pack + price | Identity attributes lock into place |
| Shopping | Editorial, product-centric | Large product imagery | Model/variant + seller cost | Side-by-side spec comparison |
| Mobility | Spatial, dynamic | Map + route | Fare + ETA + ride class | Quotes arrive around route |
| Travel | Cinematic but precise | Route/destination image | Fare + itinerary + baggage | Route arc/timeline reveal |
| Finance | Restrained, premium | Product/issuer identity | Fees + benefit + eligibility | Cost/benefit breakdown |
| SaaS | Structured, modern | Brand/product identity | Plan + seats + annual TCO | Billing cadence transforms |

### Grocery

Grocery should feel **fresh and extremely fast**.

Real product packshots matter here because identity is visual. A user should instantly see:

```text
Amul Taaza
500 ml
Pouch
```

rather than deciphering a long title.

The product card should therefore emphasize:

```text
